  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 340 PM Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... This afternoon will likely be the day with the \ngreatest shower and thunderstorm coverage out of the next seven \ndays. There are threats for both excessive rainfall as well as \ndamaging wind gusts.\n\nShowers and isolated thunderstorms continue to slowly expand in \ncoverage from east to west. Nearly all locations are forecast to \nreceive at least some minimal precipitation. The wind through the \nvertical column of the atmosphere is minimal, which means both that \nit will be hard for storms to become organized for any sort of \nwidespread severe potential in addition to showers/storms moving \nslowly. The combination of slow moving showers/storms and very moist \nair (precipitable water values >= 2 inches) is the reason that the \nentire forecast area is under a marginal (level 1 of 4) risk for \nexcessive rainfall. It is possible that a stationary front, \nextending generally from Roanoke Rapids to Wadesboro, could serve as \na focus for showers. In addition, there is a marginal (level 1 of 5) \nrisk for severe weather this afternoon across western counties, \nprimarily driven by a closed upper low over Georgia. Instability \nvalues should be slightly higher closer to the low, and while there \nis a high overcast sky across much of the region, the low could help \ntrigger some stronger storms. Coverage of showers and thunderstorms \nwill begin to diminish around sunset, with minimal showers occurring \nafter midnight.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Daily scattered showers and thunderstorms are \nexpected to continue from Wednesday through Friday, mainly each \nafternoon through evening. Coverage may become more isolated this \nweekend into early next week.\n\nThe closed mid/upper low over GA will weaken and devolve into an \nopen wave over the central/southern Appalachians on Wednesday, then \na flat shear axis by late week as it gets absorbed by the broad \ntrough over the Great Lakes. Meanwhile the subtropical high over the \nWestern Atlantic will inch its way westward, getting closer to the \nMid-Atlantic coast each day. Thus shower and storm coverage should \ngenerally be less compared to today, mostly isolated to scattered in \nnature, with the greatest coverage over the NW (in closest proximity \nto the trough) and perhaps a secondary maximum in the SE (in closest \nproximity to the sea breeze). Still, deep and persistent SSW flow \nwill continue to result in PW values about 100-125% of normal and \nmoderate instability. Thus localized spots could have rainfall rates \nof 1-2\"/hr, and WPC has at least part if not most of central NC in a \nmarginal (level 1 of 4) risk for excessive rainfall each day.\n\nThe Great Lakes trough will begin to shift east and flatten this \nweekend into early next week, as the subtropical high extends \nfarther west to the Southeast US coast. This will result in more \nzonal flow across the Eastern US and possibly a bit lower convective \nchances, but there will still be some moisture and instability \naround. So POPs are mainly low chance this weekend and slight by \nearly next week.\n\nHigh temperatures will be mainly in the mid-to-upper-80s through \nthis weekend, except perhaps some lower-90s in the far south. \nForecast highs increase to lower-to-mid-90s early next week, and \nwith continued humid conditions, heat indices in the lower-100s will \nbe possible across the south and east.",
